.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_PG_CRON 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_pg_cron
.Nd pg_cron support in pg_laswell: scheduled jobs as intents
.Sh DESCRIPTION
pg_cron support is a
.Em build-time module ,
like Citus support
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_citus 7 .
It adds two intent kinds, which schedule and unschedule pg_cron jobs, and one
refusal for the core
.Cm create_extension .
A job is then part of a signed specification and the ledger, like the schema
its command calls, instead of something run by hand.
.Pp
A binary that carries the module says so:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pg_laswell_mcp --version
0.1.3
modules: citus,pg_cron,pgvector,timescaledb
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Versions.
Measured on PostgreSQL 18.6 with pg_cron 1.6.8.
.Sh WHERE PG_CRON LIVES
pg_cron is loaded per server, through
.Va shared_preload_libraries ,
and installed in
.Em one
database per server, the one
.Va cron.database_name
names
.Pq Ql postgres
unless it is set.
.Ic CREATE EXTENSION pg_cron
anywhere else fails:
.Qq can only create extension in database postgres .
Jobs are kept in that database's
.Li cron.job
and each one runs in whichever database it names.
.Pp
Both layouts are supported, and the specification does not choose between
them: the module reads which one the server uses.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It pg_cron in the application database
.Li cron.database_name = app .
Specifications targeting
.Li app
schedule jobs directly, in the same transaction as the rest of the
specification and its ledger row: a job and the function it calls are applied
together or not at all.
.It pg_cron in a maintenance database
.Li cron.database_name = postgres ,
the default.
Jobs can only be scheduled from
.Li postgres .
A specification targeting
.Li app
that schedules one is refused, and the refusal names
.Li postgres .
The jobs go in a specification of their own whose
.Va target.connection
is a connection to
.Li postgres ,
with
.Va database
naming where each job runs, and
.Va depends_on
naming the specification that creates what the command calls:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "laswell_spec_version": 1,
  "id": "0042-nightly-purge",
  "description": "Purge expired sessions every night.",
  "target": { "connection": "cron" },
  "depends_on": [ "0041-purge-function" ],
  "intents": [
    { "kind": "pg_cron_schedule", "name": "purge-sessions",
      "schedule": "0 3 * * *", "database": "app",
      "command": "CALL app.purge_expired_sessions()" } ] }
.Ed
.Pp
That specification is applied, and recorded, in
.Li postgres ,
which needs its own ledger
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Sx BOOTSTRAP .
pg_laswell does not reach from one database into another: a specification is
the unit of atomicity, and there is no transaction across two databases.
.El
.Sh INTENT KINDS
.\" BEGIN PG_CRON KIND LIST -- checked by tools/check-manual.py against
.\" cpp/src/modules/pg_cron/kinds.inc.
Both kinds accept an optional
.Va comment ,
a note for whoever reads the specification; pg_cron has nowhere to store it.
A job is identified as pg_cron identifies it, by its name and the role it runs
as, never by its id, which comes from a sequence and differs on every server.
.Ss Cm pg_cron_schedule
Schedules a job, or changes one with the same name and role in place: pg_cron
replaces it and keeps its id.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va name
Required.
pg_cron allows a job without a name, but it cannot be found again, so a second
run would schedule it twice.
.It Va schedule
Required.
Cron syntax
.Pq Ql 0 3 * * * ,
or
.Ql N seconds
for N from 1 to 59.
Checked by pg_cron itself, which the dry run executes:
.Qq invalid schedule .
Times are in
.Va cron.timezone
.Pq GMT unless set .
.It Va command
Required.
SQL, run as given at the schedule.
.It Va database
Where the job runs.
Default: the database this specification targets.
.It Va username
The role the job runs as.
Default: the role applying the specification.
Another role needs a superuser:
.Qq must be superuser to create a job for another role .
The role must exist, may log in, and may connect to the job's database, which
pg_cron checks when the job is scheduled and the plan checks first:
.Qq role \&"x\&" does not exist ,
.Qq Jobs may only be run by roles that have the LOGIN attribute ,
.Qq User x does not have CONNECT privilege on app .
.It Va active
Default
.Li true .
.Li false
schedules the job without running it.
.El
.Pp
Already scheduled with the same schedule, command, database and
.Va active :
satisfied.
Otherwise one statement: the same transaction class as an ordinary change, no
table lock, rehearsed by executing it and rolling it back, which leaves nothing
behind (measured).
.Pp
The statement is
.Fn cron.schedule
when the job runs in this database, as the applying role, active, and
.Fn cron.schedule_in_database
otherwise.
The first is granted to PUBLIC.
The second is not:
.Qq permission denied for function schedule_in_database
until a superuser runs
.Bd -literal -offset indent
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION
  cron.schedule_in_database(text,text,text,text,text,boolean) TO runner;
.Ed
and the plan says so beforehand.
The role must also be allowed to connect to the job's database:
.Qq User runner does not have CONNECT privilege on app .
.Pp
The command itself is not checked: pg_cron stores it as text and first runs it
at its schedule.
A failure is recorded in
.Li cron.job_run_details ,
not in the plan, and the plan warns that this is so.
.Ss Cm pg_cron_unschedule
Removes a job, by
.Va name
and optional
.Va username .
Absent: satisfied, because unscheduling a name that does not exist raises
.Qq could not find valid entry for job .
A role that is not a superuser sees only its own jobs
.Po
.Li cron.job
has row-level security on
.Li username = CURRENT_USER
.Pc ,
so another role's job is refused rather than reported missing.
.\" END PG_CRON KIND LIST
.Sh READINGS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It absent
The library is not loaded:
.Va cron.database_name
is not a setting.
Every pg_cron kind is refused, naming the prerequisite.
.It Va installed No false
Loaded; this is not pg_cron's database, or it is and the extension has not been
created.
Read with
.Va database_name ,
.Va current_database ,
.Va current_user
and
.Va superuser ,
which is enough to say where to go.
.It Va installed No true
Also
.Va version ,
.Va can_schedule_in_database ,
every database with whether the applying role may connect to it, the jobs the
applying role can see keyed by role and name, and:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va roles
For a superuser only, since only a superuser may schedule for another role:
every role, whether it may log in, and the databases it may connect to.
.It Va last_runs
Each job's last run, from the most recent 1000 runs only:
.Li cron.job_run_details
grows until something purges it and is indexed on
.Va runid
and nothing else (measured), so a reading per job would scan all of it.
.It Va applied_steps
The pg_cron steps this database's ledger records as applied, newest first,
read by core from
.Li laswell.job
when the ledger exists and the applying role may read it.
.El
.El
.Pp
.Va cron.database_name
itself needs the privileges of
.Li pg_read_all_settings :
measured, a role without them gets
.Qq permission denied to examine \&"cron.database_name\&"
even from
.Fn current_setting
with
.Va missing_ok .
The reading is taken on every plan against a server that loads pg_cron, so it
checks first: an ordinary specification plans for any role, and a pg_cron intent
from such a role, outside pg_cron's database, names the grant it needs.
.Sh REFUSALS
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Cm create_extension
of
.Ql pg_cron
outside the database
.Va cron.database_name
names, or on a server that has not loaded it
.Pq Qq unrecognized configuration parameter \&"cron.database_name\&" .
.It
A job scheduled from a database that is not pg_cron's.
.It
A job for another role, by a role that is not a superuser.
.It
A job in a database that does not exist, or that the applying role may not
connect to.
.It
.Fn cron.schedule_in_database
without
.Li EXECUTE
on it.
.It
A job for a role that does not exist, cannot log in, or may not connect to the
job's database.
.El
.Sh ADVISORIES
Facts that move on their own, shown with the plan as
.Ql note:
and left out of
.Va planDigest
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Sx planMigration .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It A job edited by hand
.Li cron.job
is a table the job's role can change \(em
.Fn cron.alter_job ,
.Fn cron.unschedule ,
a second
.Fn cron.schedule
\(em so a job can drift from the specification that declared it with no
migration involved.
Each
.Cm pg_cron_schedule
step records what it declared, and the ledger keeps it.
On
.Em any
plan against pg_cron's database \(em not only when the same specification is
planned again \(em the newest declaration of each job is compared with
.Li cron.job ,
and a job whose schedule, command, database or
.Va active
differs, that has gone, or that came back after a
.Cm pg_cron_unschedule
is named.
A role that is not a superuser sees only its own jobs, so only those are
compared.
Every released binary records the declaration: pg_cron first shipped in
0.1.3-alpha2, already recording it.
A step without one \(em applied by a build between releases \(em is not
compared.
.It A job whose last run failed
For each job the specification schedules or unschedules: the failure message,
and how many of its runs among the most recent 1000 failed.
.El
.Sh WHY THERE IS NO ALTER_JOB KIND
.Cm pg_cron_schedule
already changes a job in place: pg_cron replaces a job of the same name and role
and keeps its id (measured).
.Fn cron.alter_job
would be a second way to make the same change, and a worse one: it is not
granted to PUBLIC, so the role that owns a job may reschedule it with
.Fn cron.schedule
and may not alter it.
To pause a job, schedule it with
.Va active
false.
